  "ai_show_review": {
    "summary": "Lovelight fireworks, first Ripple, lively acoustic half, solid electric jam",
    "blurb": "The acoustic half showcases How Long Blues, Candyman and the debut of Ripple with bright piano from Pigpen. The electric second set drives forward with China Cat Sunflower > I Know You Rider, multiple Good Lovin' renditions and a scorching Turn On Your Lovelight. Overall playing is tight, energetic and full of historic firsts.",
    "review": "On August 19, 1970 at the Fillmore West the Dead delivered a two‑part marathon that still feels fresh decades later. The first set leans heavily on acoustic textures – Pigpen’s upright piano adds a crisp, percussive backbone to How Long Blues and Candyman, while Jerry’s expressive guitar and pedal‑steel weave around the band’s tight rhythm section. The highlight is the very first live performance of \"Ripple\", which glides effortlessly into the warm harmonies of \"Brokedown Palace\".\n\nThe electric second set kicks off with a bright \"Cold Rain And Snow\" before launching into a classic mid‑show flow: \"China Cat Sunflower\" segues into \"I Know You Rider\", then rolls through \"Saint Stephen\" and a powerhouse \"Good Lovin'\" sandwich around the drum duet. The climactic \"Not Fade Away > Turn On Your Lovelight\" sequence is blistering, with Jerry’s soaring solos, Phil’s lively bass lines, and Pigpen’s piano punctuating the jam. Though captured from audience tapes, the best recording (Taback’s cassette) offers surprisingly clear separation and vivid mids that let every instrument shine.",
